      <li><a href="/openshift-docs-markdown/operators/admin/olm-creating-policy#olm-creating-policy">Allowing non-cluster administrators to install Operators</a></li>
      <li><a href="/openshift-docs-markdown/operators/admin/olm-managing-custom-catalogs#olm-restricted-networks-operatorhub_olm-managing-custom-catalogs">Disabling the default OperatorHub catalog sources</a></li>
      </ul>
      </section>
      <h2 id="olm-colocation_olm-multitenancy" class="group scroll-mt-20">Operator colocation and Operator groups<a href="#olm-colocation_olm-multitenancy" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p>Operator Lifecycle Manager (OLM) handles OLM-managed Operators that are installed in the same namespace, meaning their <code>Subscription</code> resources are colocated in the same namespace, as related Operators. Even if they are not actually related, OLM considers their states, such as their version and update policy, when any one of them is updated.</p>
